I suppose I should explain my reasoning.  In order from least to
most compelling...

1. The version you gave, with "Liddle" and "Lamzy"
   beginning with L, is the first version I've ever seen
   like that.  Previously, I had only encountered versions
   with D in that position, suggesting that the rhyme
   predated the sound shift that brought us our current
   alveolar D.  (Previously, D was a true dental, which
   is phonetically much closer to L.)  Since the current
   pronunciation of D is standard throughout the
   English-speaking world, this suggested a date prior
   to the colonial era.

   However, now that I am aware that there are
   versions of the rhyme with L, this argument is
   much less compelling.  Once the rhyme was
   written, the Ls could easily have shifted to D
   via assimilation (extending the alliteration from
   the earlier portion), particularly if people misheard
   the rhyme initially before realizing the implied
   meaning.

2. The reference to mares eating oats implies that the
   automobile was not yet in widespread use among the
   middle class, which implies a date prior to about the
   mid twenties.  (The lower class could not afford to
   feed oats to an animal in any case.  The upper class
   could and still do, but while they might have horses
   around for pretentious reasons they wouldn't have
   other barnyard animals, especially sheep, which are
   fairly unpleasant if you get up close and personal
   with them.  The middle class stopped buying oats
   to feed to horses when they got cars.)

3. The reference to humans eating ivy ("Wouldn't
   you?") implies that the word "ivy" had not yet
   been narrowed to its current rather limited range
   of meaning.  None of the plants that have been
   called ivy since the seventeenth century or so
   are edible for humans.

4. The use of the word "doe" to refer to what in
   context clearly must be a domestic animal,
   without giving any other indication as to what
   kind of animal that might be, is right properly
   old.  I cannot find another citation for this usage
   since the time of Chaucer.  (The word "doe" is
   occasionally used for female rabbits even in
   modern times, but only in a context where the
   reader or listener already knows you're talking
   about rabbits.  Otherwise, in all modern usage,
   the word "doe" is particular to female deer,
   which to the best of my knowledge have
   never been successfully domesticated.)  This,
   to my way of thinking, really nails it.  It is
   virtually impossible to imagine a nineteenth-
   much less a twentieth-century writer coming
   up with the idea that does eat oats.


